*Heading
 Net pull-out, ALTERNATED weave -- JOB 5/5 of the restart chain: Phase E (PULLOUT) -- quadratic C3D10M, alpha=1000 damping (beta=0)
** ==========================================================================
** Restart-continuation job: picks up the full model state at the end of
** job 4 (net_pullout_phaseD_free).
**
** Submit with:
**   abaqus job=net_pullout_phaseE_pullout oldjob=net_pullout_phaseD_free \
**          input=net_pullout_phaseE_pullout.inp interactive
**
** Pull the central x-beam's RIGHT clamp a further +30 mm (cumulative
** from its nominal, post-release position) over 1.2 s. The left clamp
** (freed in job 4) is left untouched here and continues to evolve under
** elastic/contact forces only. SMOOTH_E is the amplitude declared in
** job A's model data.
**
** Three restart-chain behaviours worth knowing before editing this deck:
** (1) Across a restart job boundary, a plain *Boundary card is ADDITIVE
**     to the whole carried-forward BC history, not a modification of a
**     matching entry, even when it reuses the same set name. Any
**     restart-continuation job that touches *Boundary needs op=NEW,
**     restating the full table (as below).
** (2) *Restart, read, step=N uses the GLOBAL CUMULATIVE step number
**     across the whole chain (A=1, B=2, C=3, D=4, E=5), not a count
**     relative to oldjob=. A fresh run from job D therefore reads
**     step=4; resuming a crashed run of job E itself (oldjob=this same
**     job) would instead need step=5.
** (3) *Output, history must be restated in any job that also redeclares
**     *Output, field (as here, to get a finer field cadence than job
**     A's) -- redeclaring one silently resets the ENTIRE inherited
**     output-request state, including the energy and node-history
**     requests. Jobs B/C/D never touch *Output and so correctly keep
**     inheriting job A's full request.
**
** This is the longest step (1.2 s) and involves the most sliding contact
** activity, so intra-step restart checkpoints every 0.1 s (12 across the
** step) limit how much a crash late in the pull would cost to redo.
** ==========================================================================
*Restart, read, step=4
**
*Step, name=PULLOUT
*Dynamic, Explicit
 , 1.2
** dt lowered 1.5e-6 -> 1.0e-7 (2026-10-21): disables mass scaling
** entirely for this mesh (its own natural floor is 1.31e-7 s) -- see
** net_pullout_phaseA_engage.inp's ENGAGE step for why the old
** target crashed (mass-scaling x new Rayleigh damping interaction).
*Fixed Mass Scaling, dt=1.5e-06, type=below min
** Tightened 12 -> 60 (2026-10-21, overnight-interruption resilience): at
** the ~6.6 hr/0.1s-step-time rate observed in Phase A, the old spacing
** (every 0.1s of step time) risked losing ~6.6 hours per missed
** checkpoint across this phase's multi-day runtime. Now every 0.02s of
** step time, ~1.3 hr worst case (Phase E's own rate is still unobserved
** and may differ once sliding contact is active -- treat this as an
** estimate, not a guarantee).
*Restart, write, number interval=60
**
*Boundary, op=NEW, amplitude=SMOOTH_E
